Part II: Blink LED (baremetal)
•	Figure 1 and 2
•	Explanation:
o	STM32CubeMX – Starting the project with STM32CubeMX allows the user to configure individual pins, which was a key component of this lab. While beginning the project by selecting the correct board in the software will configure all the correct timers, etc. It does not default to correctly configuring GPIO pins, which is necessary for LED Toggling. 
o	STM32CubeIDE – After the pin was configured and the corresponding code was generated, the correct lines of code needed to be added to the while loop to run infinitely. The code in the while(1) loop in Figure 1 shows a toggle of GPIO Pin 5 on Port A every 1000ms. This was done using the HAL embedded library that allows for direct interface with the GPIO pins.
Part III: Serial output
•	Figure 1 and 2, Code: Pg. 11-17
•	Explanation:
o	STM32CubeMX – As mentioned, starting the project with STM32CubeMX allows the user to configure individual pins, as it does not default to correctly configuring UART pins, which is necessary for the serial output to occur. For this, Pin 2 on Port A was configured to be the UART Transmitter and Pin 3 on Port A was configured to be the UART receiver.
o	STM32CubeIDE – After the pins were configured and the corresponding code was generated, the correct lines of code needed to be added to the while loop to run infinitely. Under the code that toggles the LED in the while(1) loop, is the correct structure to output the “Hello World!!!” character string seen in the serial monitoring terminal. Similar to the LED toggle, the HAL library was used to transmit the string over serial communication
Part IV: Blink LED using RTOS
•	Figure 3 and 4, Code: Pg. 12-18
•	Explanation:
o	STM32CubeMX – Previously, CubeMX was used for configuring pins. For this, the “FreeRTOS” kernel is needed to define RTOS tasks that will handle the blinking rather than plan script in a super loop. 2 Tasks were defined in the respective fields, two tasks that will each toggle the LED. One task with a higher priority over the other, to create an imbalance in the frequency each task is called upon to execute.
o	STM32CubeIDE – After the tasks were configured and the corresponding code was generated, instead of writing code in the super loop. The code is written in the task definition that is generated by CubeMX. Again using the HAL library to do this, with each task to execute the same function (‘Toggle Pin’). **Observation** Changing priorities of the tasks create a different pattern of blinking because of the timing and frequency each task is called. 
